WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.147 --> 00:00:10.588
Nobody wants to get sick.

2
00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.251
Diseases can be scary, and some have no cure.

3
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:23.074
Today we can prevent many diseases, but in earlier times people were not so lucky.

4
00:00:24.314 --> 00:00:28.056
Smallpox, for example, plagued people for thousands of years.

5
00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:35.338
Learning how to prevent this disease led to the development of vaccination, which now prevents many more diseases.

6
00:00:37.218 --> 00:00:39.499
Smallpox was a highly contagious illness.

7
00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:45.161
It usually started with fever and vomiting, and soon a rash would appear.

8
00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:50.222
The rash eventually formed scabs that left horrible scars when they fell off.

9
00:00:52.443 --> 00:00:56.504
Smallpox also blinded people and killed 30 percent of its victims.

10
00:00:58.428 --> 00:01:01.789
Smallpox was just one of many illnesses caused by viruses.

11
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.552
Viruses are extremely tiny organisms that take over living cells.

12
00:01:08.932 --> 00:01:12.574
To reproduce, they force those cells to make more of the virus.

13
00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:16.415
Ancient people didn't know about viruses.

14
00:01:18.256 --> 00:01:22.858
But they did notice that people who survived smallpox usually didn't get it again.

15
00:01:24.178 --> 00:01:27.920
They were immune to smallpox even if they were re-exposed to it.

16
00:01:29.068 --> 00:01:33.551
By the end of the first millennium, the Chinese had developed a way to prevent people from getting

17
00:01:33.631 --> 00:01:40.454
smallpox. They crushed smallpox scabs into a powder and inhaled it through the nose.

18
00:01:41.655 --> 00:01:47.338
This deliberate exposure to smallpox was less dangerous than catching the disease and offered protection from it.

19
00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:52.221
Similar techniques of exposure were also used in India and Turkey.

20
00:01:53.702 --> 00:01:57.504
Inhaling smallpox scabs wasn't a perfect method of preventing the disease.

21
00:01:59.196 --> 00:02:00.656
Some people became sick from it.

22
00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:03.937
Over the years the practice evolved.

23
00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:09.719
Rather than snorting the smallpox virus, people began placing it in an open cut.

24
00:02:11.799 --> 00:02:16.581
They also figured out that scabs from people with mild smallpox were the safest to use.

25
00:02:18.081 --> 00:02:21.322
This method wasn't practiced in Europe until the eighteenth century.

26
00:02:22.562 --> 00:02:28.404
Lady Mary Wortley Montagu, the wife of England's ambassador to Turkey, became a leading advocate of the practice.

27
00:02:30.324 --> 00:02:34.705
She had learned of it while living in Turkey and had her own child receive the procedure.

28
00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:41.528
After that, the practice became more common, although some people remained reluctant to try it.

29
00:02:43.248 --> 00:02:48.870
Exposure to the live smallpox virus was still risky.

30
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:54.972
In 1796 an English physician and scientist named Edward Jenner developed a safer way to prevent smallpox.

31
00:02:56.876 --> 00:03:02.521
He had noticed that local milkmaids often got cowpox, a similar but much milder illness.

32
00:03:03.982 --> 00:03:07.265
After they got cowpox, they were protected from smallpox too.

33
00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:13.911
Jenner experimented, wondering if he could come up with a safer way to prevent smallpox.

34
00:03:15.373 --> 00:03:18.635
Eventually this led him to the idea of vaccination.

35
00:03:20.357 --> 00:03:26.086
Vaccination uses a dead or weakened version of a virus, which is less dangerous than the live virus

36
00:03:26.286 --> 00:03:27.627
used in earlier procedures.

37
00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:35.693
After exposure to a virus, the immune system creates antibodies, substances that fight infection and prevent illness.

38
00:03:37.574 --> 00:03:42.298
Later, if the body sees that virus again, it will recognize it and know to make more antibodies.

39
00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.863
This is how the body develops immunity to fight diseases naturally.

40
00:03:50.568 --> 00:03:55.933
The weaker viruses used in vaccines get the body to make antibodies but don't make a person ill.

41
00:03:57.514 --> 00:04:00.777
Jenner's smallpox vaccinations proved safe and effective.

42
00:04:02.438 --> 00:04:06.322
Governments quickly worked to vaccinate entire populations against smallpox.

43
00:04:07.823 --> 00:04:12.988
Soon the number of smallpox cases and deaths fell significantly in some parts of the world.

44
00:04:14.429 --> 00:04:17.692
The knowledge gained from Jenner's method wasn't limited to smallpox.

45
00:04:19.412 --> 00:04:26.114
In the late 1800s, French scientist Louis Pasteur's studies of germs and disease helped him develop a vaccine

46
00:04:26.174 --> 00:04:26.735
for rabies.

47
00:04:28.475 --> 00:04:34.097
Over the century, scientists developed vaccines for other diseases as well, but progress was slow.

48
00:04:35.297 --> 00:04:42.700
By the 1920s scientists achieved more breakthroughs and more diseases could be prevented by vaccination, including diphtheria.

49
00:04:44.424 --> 00:04:50.887
In the 1950s researchers developed a vaccine for polio, a disease that had left thousands of children and

50
00:04:50.927 --> 00:04:52.048
adults paralyzed.

51
00:04:53.809 --> 00:04:57.391
Smallpox still remained a threat despite the development of the vaccine.

52
00:04:58.691 --> 00:05:04.154
Throughout the first half of the twentieth century, many nations had wiped out smallpox entirely.

53
00:05:05.355 --> 00:05:07.736
But outbreaks still occurred in developing nations.

54
00:05:09.992 --> 00:05:17.237
In 1966 the World Health Organization began a project of mass vaccination to eliminate smallpox in the places

55
00:05:17.277 --> 00:05:18.318
where it still existed.

56
00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:22.841
By 1980 the illness had been completely eradicated.

57
00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:28.545
Today children can receive vaccinations to protect them from different illnesses.

58
00:05:29.926 --> 00:05:32.588
But children aren't the only ones who have to get vaccinated.

59
00:05:33.929 --> 00:05:37.332
Many childhood vaccinations require more shots later in life.

60
00:05:39.424 --> 00:05:42.446
These shots are like a recharge for the original vaccine.

61
00:05:43.726 --> 00:05:48.509
Each year people of all ages get flu shots for protection against the influenza virus.

62
00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.713
The flu virus changes quickly, so scientists must create a new vaccine each year.

63
00:05:57.534 --> 00:06:01.496
The flu vaccine prevents over a million cases of flu each year.

64
00:06:02.757 --> 00:06:06.218
Smallpox is no longer a threat to humans thanks to vaccines.

65
00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:14.039
However, new diseases have emerged with no treatments, cures, or vaccines available.

66
00:06:15.321 --> 00:06:19.007
Perhaps the lessons learned from smallpox will prevent future illnesses.

67
00:06:20.609 --> 00:06:25.136
While vaccines have saved millions of lives, there are still more lives to be saved.
